[Music]  i was scrolling back in time  recently past the billions of little  moments  i've lived all these photos that i've  captured over the years  and i stumbled upon a region of my photo  bank that i  hadn't really ever looked at before it  was like right here  we had just gotten married and we walked  from our little apartment  up to the mountain near our house how  you doing  we had no idea if we could actually get  to the top or how long it would take  but we hiked we camped at the top  and we watched the sun fall over the  mountain  and i just love looking at these photos  [Music]  a few weeks later iz surprised me with a  road trip to san diego and then into  tijuana  crossing the border to tijuana i had  completely forgotten  about this experience we spent so many  hours on the beach playing frisbee we  ate tacos we visited  old friends there was this political  rally that we wandered into  man i just i i don't  even remember that and then suddenly i  do  that was quote one of the happiest days  of my life  okay  when i look at these photos at these  videos  something happens in my brain  it's like i'm mourning something and  yet celebrating it too i've been  thinking about this feeling for a while  and i've been talking to my friend  nathaniel drew who also  is very interested in investigating this  so hold on do you wanna i feel like i'm  overthinking this but i want you to be  happy with the framing that i've got  at least frame rate i'm shooting in 24.  that's video  dude we can't help ourselves talk about  composition and frame rate  right up top but yeah no we're  my theory on nostalgia is that  nostalgia is kind of like fire okay and  it can be kind of like playing fire so  fire can it can warm you up but  i feel like you can get lost in it too  staring too long at the fire getting too  close you can get burned  as we talked we sort of laid out our  theories of  what's going on here and then we decided  how we could investigate this further by  experimenting on ourselves  what would happen to my brain if i  started listening to  music from 2005  and watching old home videos  and smelling old smells and  just sparking that feeling every day  if i may just make a guess here i do  wonder if this will lead to  you kind of returning to your roots  whatever that means so  nathaniel and i set out on separate  journeys both of us trying to spark  a certain type of nostalgia in our own  way  nathaniel's goal was to go back in time  and start to experience the world in a  more analog  way without social media and phones and  etc and for me  the journey was going to be getting out  old journals  old books old smells old music  and cultivating a practice for every day  i expose myself  to this old stuff and see what that does  to my brain  i dived into the neuroscience and looked  at the studies that have been done on  what happens to the brain  but i also looked at my own personal  experience and i learned a lot  and that's what i want to share with you  today  so i have a theory on how all of us  experience  life it looks like this  okay so you were born here my theory is  that as you grow up you start  experiencing the world like this  this little ring represents the stuff  that you're thinking about  worrying about excited about this ring  stretches  maybe a few days back and forward in  time  maybe a week or two but not much more  than that  if you were living totally in the  present moment it would look something  like this  if you were constantly thinking about  everything everything that has happened  over the course of your entire year or  your life  the ring would look a lot more like this  but i believe most people's conscious  experience looks like this  thinking about the good and stressful  things that happened yesterday at work  or worrying about the good and stressful  things that are coming up tomorrow  or maybe next week we have a productive  day at work  and then you have a really good  conversation with your sister on the  phone one evening  and then you also get in a fight with  your partner  and i believe that most of us use the  information in this ring  to construct the current story of  ourselves  in other words we use this information  to decide what we think and  feel about ourselves who we think we are  but as we move forward these events will  fade and  new events will come into the ring and  help us construct a new story  of who we think we are what we feel  about ourselves  [Music]  so you're moving along and then one  night something really good happens  you go out with friends you dance you  have so much fun  it's the first week of october and the  air is turning cool and crisp  it smells like fall it smells like the  city it smells like liquor like perfume  like  freedom you have this one conversation  that is so  exciting so inspiring you feel connected  to this place to these people  and then it's over and for a few days  you consciously  remember that night you fondly think  about it but a week later  you kind of have no reason to think  about it anymore that night sort of  starts to fade  those conversations that feeling those  smells those memories are still stored  away  but your conscious mind has new stuff to  put its spotlight on  that night as a memory as an experience  gets filed away in some dusty file  cabinet somewhere in the depths of your  long-term memory  and then suddenly it's a time in your  life that looks  so different from that great night you  had with friends  you haven't seen friends in a long time  you're lonely you're wondering if you've  even ever had  friends you're wondering if you're even  likable  you're going off the information that's  available to you  the stuff that's in this ring which on  both sides  is full of dull quiet loneliness  but what if somehow you could retrieve  that knight from the old dusty file  cabinet in your long-term memory  you dust it off you maybe edit it a  little bit  so that it just includes the best parts  and then you bring it forth  and put it in the ring something happens  to your brain  when you do this  suddenly you have more information to  add to your story about yourself  this information is old information but  it feels  new because you haven't thought about it  in a really long time and it challenges  your current  story the story about you being lonely  and unlikable  and in this moment you feel two things  really strongly  you feel a literal physical warmness  a wonder a comfort a happiness and  belonging  but the other feeling you get is a  yearning to  be back in that moment and an  acknowledgement that it will never  happen again  these two feelings are the ingredients  for the potent  experience of nostalgia  [Music]  so when neuroscientists look at the  brain of people who are experiencing  nostalgia  they see something pretty interesting  they see these  two spots that fire at once and sort of  start talking to each other  a very unique process in the brain this  one  is the mind recalling the memory it's  technically called  chronological remoteness let's just call  it memory and this one  is your emotional network the stuff that  makes you  feel happy or sad and during nostalgia  specifically  it's firing in the reward centers the  good  the positive yes brain likes this  these two fire together and connect with  each other  it's a strange neural dance between  these two  unlikely partners of memory and  emotional reward  this feeling and reaction is more potent  if  one the memory has to do with belonging  or personal  attachment like friends and family or if  the feeling was a compelling  impactful moment in your life that had a  lot of personal meaning  maybe a big career thing or a big  life-changing moment  and number two the feeling is more  intense the longer it's been  since you thought about it so like that  feeling with me  on the beach in tijuana i literally had  not  thought about that in eight years and so  looking at those photos it was a  compelling  personal meaningful moment my brain was  going wild  the studies on this don't just look at  the fmris and the  brain and all of that stuff which is  really interesting  but it also looks at what actually  happens in the current day life of the  person who is experiencing nostalgia  what some of these studies are finding  is that nostalgia can actually  increase somebody's personal view of  themselves their self-esteem  and it can help combat the effects of  loneliness  as i cultivated nostalgia i really  started to understand why that is and  how that actually works  i am listening to weezer to an album  that came out when i was a senior in  high school  and it is just flooding my  world with this feeling  one of the things that keeps coming back  to me is the people  the people i was with like the the  friday nights  i would have with my friends the place  is there  in my memory but the people i was with  i feel like their presence in some  strange way  as i cultivate these memories it's  really weird  i like see their faces i like feel like  i'm with them  again it's like good and warm but it's  also  like hurtful like i found myself wanting  to turn this music off because i was  like oh i don't want to go there right  now  it's very it's very contradictory these  feelings  anyway back to it this is in keeping  with my broader theory about  the stories we tell ourselves and how  the information in our little ring  is usually the information we use to  construct our conscious story  and that introducing old memories that  are positive or meaningful  or tell a different story than your  current one can  really help you think differently about  who you are and what your life is  your conscious story becomes richer  and more flush with good experiences  and positive information about you and  your life  so while i was doing this nathaniel was  also doing it on his side  but in a very different way we're off to  a bad start here  where are we i have no idea we're here  actually  the last time i remember ever even  holding a map like this  was with my mom 15 years ago  okay but nostalgia has a dark side too  one of the big takeaways from the  studies on nostalgia  find that people actually lean on  nostalgia during hard times  they'll use it as sort of a coping  mechanism and this sometimes can be  exploited by marketers or in a lot of  cases  politicians during the old days when a  crime was a crime  we will make america great again  this happens in so many different  contexts to the far-right  nationalistic movements in japan that  are hearkening back to  the japanese empire and how much better  it was you can also look at  isis and their focus on hearkening back  to the glory days  of the old caliphate it happened here in  this country in 2016  with make america great again hearkening  back to some  ill-defined fake censored past that  never really existed  but that helps people who are maybe  struggling with  changing demographics in the country or  losing power  to feel like they have something to  latch on to these political campaigns  exploit that same brain process that  i experience when i look at old photos  but instead  they utilize it to mobilize supporters  [Music]  so i did this for a month i cultivated  nostalgia  every day listened to old music  and read journals and looked at old  photos and footage  and i can say that i definitely learned  a lot so during these strange  times of loneliness and non-social  interaction  remember that your ring is full of  information that  is not super indicative of who you  really are  if you're feeling lonely or disconnected  i recommend you experiment with taking  the edge off  with a dose of nostalgia  um i would probably want to meet  one of the guys like leonardo da vinci  that were really  smart and really good thanks for  watching everybody  this was quite the journey and  i have all these journals back here  maybe too much  there's such thing as over documenting  i've learned so before you go  i want to just tell you about skillshare  who sponsored today's video skillshare  is a place where you go to learn new  skills i'm a  giant fan of online learning i believe  you can open  so many creative doors if you dedicate  yourself  to taking online classes that's how i  learned all of my skills  and skillshare is a perfect place to do  that it is  incredibly cheap the first thousand  people to use the link in my description  will get a free trial of skillshare  premium this is a membership that allows  you to watch  everything on the skillshare website for  free from photography  to videography to design to animation  to entrepreneurial skills to cooking  there are so many options  what i love about skillshare is that you  pay one subscription  price and you get unlimited access to  all of the tutorials you can watch  dozens of tutorials  so go check that out there's a course on  there that was actually just made by our  friend nathaniel drew who  you saw in this video nathaniel does a  course on finding your voice as an  online creator  and it's a fantastic course he breaks  down everything he knows about  creating a personal authentic voice  as a creator on the internet nathaniel's  a very talented storyteller and  just a good teacher so go click the link  in the description when you do that it  supports this channel but  it also kickstarts a new chapter in your  life  of learning skills on the internet which  again  is a big value of mine so thank you all  for watching  and i hope you have a wonderful day  we'll see you soon

